6. Does the X-band departure saturate against NDVI? (the standing criticism of
   X-band for crop work: a 3 cm wave saturates early with crop parameters)
   bin      n   NDVI range      NDVI mean   departure dB   dB per NDVI unit
     1    136   0.223-0.334       0.288         -1.328           -
     2    135   0.334-0.396       0.364         -0.350      +12.89
     3    135   0.396-0.455       0.426         -0.051       +4.85
     4    136   0.455-0.555       0.504         +0.188       +3.07
     5    135   0.555-0.646       0.605         +1.054       +8.56
     6    136   0.646-0.824       0.701         +1.452       +4.15
   departure runs -1.33 to +1.45 dB and is MONOTONE INCREASING across all 6 bins;
   the increment is +3.07 to +12.89 dB per NDVI unit and ends at +4.15,
   so it does not collapse toward zero at the top. Over the NDVI range these
   fields actually occupy, the response does NOT saturate. That is the measured
   answer to the standing X-band criticism, and it is a bounded answer: the top
   bin averages NDVI 0.70, so this says nothing about
   biomass beyond what Sokhda grew. A compressed top end would have bounded the
   RANKING's dynamic range rather than inverted it -- the model claims a
   within-cohort rank on an external level, not a biomass retrieval.

