=== is the October correlation vegetation, or a static field property? ===
905 farms with clean optical on both 10 Jun and 13 Oct.
A time-invariant property -- surface roughness, tillage, drainage, parcel geometry --
would correlate with gamma0 on *any* pairing of dates. Vegetation would not.

  same-date pairs (the relationship replicates at a different season):
    corr(NDVI 10 Jun, gamma0  6 Jun)  = +0.531
    corr(NDVI 13 Oct, gamma0 13 Oct)  = +0.514

  cross-date pairs (a static property would keep these positive too):
    corr(NDVI 13 Oct, gamma0  6 Jun)  = +0.089
    corr(NDVI 13 Oct, gamma0 19 Jun)  = -0.173
    corr(NDVI 10 Jun, gamma0 13 Oct)  = +0.179

  DIFFERENCED — every time-invariant field property cancels:
    corr(dNDVI, dgamma0) = +0.654 pearson, +0.586 spearman;  slope +5.74 dB per NDVI unit

  Verdict: same-date pairs are strongly positive and cross-date pairs are not,
  and the differenced relationship is the strongest of all. The sign is vegetation.

  NOTE: this control was originally written expecting June to be nearly bare, so
  that a strong June correlation would indicate a soil artefact. June NDVI averages
  0.404 here, so that premise was false and the test as first framed did
  not measure what it claimed. The cross-date and differenced pairs above are the
  controls that actually separate vegetation from a static field property.

