  The village total is near-flat because the factor is CENTRED: every cohort median is
  exactly 1.0, so widening the span moves plots symmetrically about it and the sum barely
  moves. What the span sets is the per-plot SPREAD, which is what it is for. The constant
  is a statement about how much a within-cohort rank is allowed to say, not a lever on
  the answer -- and that is now measured rather than asserted.

uncertainty budget on the village total of 893.9 t -- each row is the whole chain
re-run under that one change, not a formula propagated through it:
  source                                    low t    high t   +- t    +- %   basis
  reference yield Y_ref (stated scenario)    804.5    983.3   89.4   10.0   +-10% on the DA&FW 3rd Advance Estimate
  district crop mix (allocation prior)      832.3    955.3   61.5    6.9   200 draws at sigma=0.51 in log-share, calibrated on Rice and Cotton
  crop labelling (Round 2's labels)         880.3    893.9    6.8    0.8   the whole chain re-run on the four-date labels
  speckle on the farm means                 890.1    895.6    2.7    0.3   1000 draws at 4.34/sqrt(N) dB per plot, 5-95 %
  tier-2 tie ordering                       893.9    893.9    0.0    0.0   200 permutations of the tied keys on departure_T6
  EXTERNAL assumptions (state reference + district mix) sum to 150.9 t; everything the
  radar and this pipeline contribute sums to 9.5 t.
  That is the shape a no-ground-truth forecast should have, and it is a stronger statement
  than the one this table made until 2026-08-31, when it priced the reference and omitted
  the crop mix entirely. The per-plot SAR term ranks plots within a cohort; both the level
  it ranks around AND the size of three of the five cohorts are somebody else's numbers.

  how the district mix was priced, since it is the row most open to challenge:
    two crops are assigned by threshold rules, not by the mix, so the mix can be
    scored against them -- district share vs what this village measures:
      Rice      district 0.26   measured 0.170   log-ratio -0.426
      Cotton    district 0.32   measured 0.102   log-ratio -1.147
    the SPREAD of those log-ratios, sigma=0.51, is the scale used to
    perturb the three tier-2 weights over 200 draws. A common bias renormalises
    away; what moves the split is the crops disagreeing by different amounts.
      Bajra     cohort area   13.4 -  184.2 ha
      Maize     cohort area   21.3 -  246.2 ha
      Groundnut cohort area   38.1 -  255.9 ha
    This is a SCENARIO. It assumes the prior errs on the three crops we cannot
    check by about as much as it errs on the two we can, which is an assumption --
    but a measured one, and better than assuming the prior is exact.
